00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:06.360
By now, you should have a sense of how word embeddings can help you build NLP applications.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.560
One of the most fascinating properties of word embeddings is that they can also help

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.760
with analogy reasoning.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.280
And while reasoning about analogies may not be by itself the most important NLP application,

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.640
they might also help convey a sense of what these word embeddings are doing, what these

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.720
word embeddings can do.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.420
Let me show you what I mean.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:31.140
Here are the feature-rise representations of a set of words that you might hope a word

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.420
embedding could capture.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:44.220
Let's say I pose the question, man is to woman as king is to what?

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.380
Many of you would say, man is to woman as king is to queen.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.020
But is it possible to have an algorithm figure this out automatically?

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.020
Well, here's how you could do it.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.140
Let's say that you're using this four-dimensional vector to represent man.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.300
So this would be E5391.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.980
Although just for this video, let me call this E subscript man.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.820
And let's say that's the embedding vector for woman.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.580
So I'm going to call that E subscript woman.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.260
And similarly for king and queen.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.500
And for this example, I'm just going to assume you're using four-dimensional embeddings rather

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.900
than anywhere from 50 to 1,000-dimensional, which would be more typical.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:38.290
One interesting property of these vectors is that if you take the vector E man and subtract

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:51.630
the vector E woman, then you end up with approximately minus one negative minus another

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.250
one is negative two.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.730
That's about zero minus zero, zero minus zero, close to zero minus zero.

00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.970
So you get roughly negative two, zero, zero, zero.

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:13.570
And similarly, if you take E king minus E queen, then that's approximately the same thing.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.970
That's about minus one minus 0.97.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:20.890
That's about negative two.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.730
This is about one minus one, since kings and queens are both equally royal.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:31.570
So they're zero, and then age, difference, food difference, zero.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:37.990
And so what this is capturing is that the main difference between man and woman is the

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:44.290
gender, and the main difference between king and queen, as represented by these vectors,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:49.970
is also the gender, which is why the difference, E man minus E woman, and the difference, E

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.040
king minus E queen, are about the same.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:03:01.800
So one way to carry out this analogy reasoning is, if the algorithm is asked, man is the woman

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.520
as king as to what?

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:11.280
What it can do is compute E man minus E woman, and try to find a vector, try to find a word

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:20.320
so that E man minus E woman is close to E king minus E of that new word.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.560
And it turns out that when queen is the word plugged in here, then the left hand side is

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.500
close to the right hand side.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:36.160
So these ideas were first pointed out by Thomas Mikoloff, Wen Tao Yi, and Jeffy Zweig.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:42.120
And it's been one of the most remarkable and surprising and influential results about

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.000
word embeddings.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.240
And I think has helped the whole community get better intuitions about word embeddings,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.520
about what word embeddings are doing.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.760
So let's formalize how you can turn this into an algorithm.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:03.840
In pictures, the word embeddings live in maybe a 300 dimensional space.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:11.720
And so the word man is represented as a point into space, and the word woman is represented

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.720
as a point into space.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.800
And the word king is represented as another point.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.640
And the word queen is represented as another point.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.680
And what we pointed out really on the last slide is that the vector difference between

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:37.360
man and woman is very similar to the vector difference between king and queen.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.520
And this arrow I just drew is really the vector that represents a difference in gender.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.120
And remember, these are points we're plotting in a 300 dimensional space.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.640
So in order to carry out this type of analogical reasoning to figure out man as woman, as king

00:04:53.640 --> 00:05:08.330
as the what, what you can do is try to find the word W so that this equation holds true.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:16.530
So you want, and so what you want is to find the word W that maximizes the similarity

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:38.160
between E W compared to E king minus E man plus E woman.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.880
So what I did was I took this E question mark and replaced that with E W and then brought

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:51.360
E W to just one side of the equation and then the other three terms to the right hand side

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.360
of this equation.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.040
So if you have some appropriate similarity function for measuring how similar is the embedding

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:04.120
of some word W to this quantity of the right, then finding the word that maximizes the similarity

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:11.230
should hopefully let you pick out the word queen.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:14.430
And the remarkable thing is this actually works.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.190
If you learn a set of word embeddings and find the word W that maximizes this type

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:24.630
of similarity, you can actually get the exact right answer, you know, depending on

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.630
the details of the task.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:30.870
But if you look at research papers, it's not uncommon for research papers to report anywhere

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:39.270
from say 30% to 75% accuracy on analogy reasoning tasks like these, where you count a analogy

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:44.310
attempts as correct only if it gets the exact word right.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.150
So only if in this case it picks out the word queen.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:53.190
Before moving on, I just want to clarify what this plot on the left is.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:58.990
So previously we talked about using algorithms like T-SNE to visualize words.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:07.870
What T-SNE does is it takes 300D data and it maps it in a very nonlinear way to a 2D

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:09.430
space.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:15.030
And so the mapping that T-SNE learns, this is a very complicated and very nonlinear mapping.

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:21.670
So after the T-SNE mapping, you should not expect these types of parallelogram relationships

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:25.670
like the one we saw on the left to hold true.

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:31.150
And it's really in this original 300 dimensional space that you could more reliably count on

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.830
these types of parallelogram relationships in analogy pairs to hold true.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:42.870
And it may hold true after mapping through T-SNE, but in most cases, because of T-SNE's

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.870
nonlinear mapping, you should not count on that.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:51.310
And many of the parallelogram analogy relationships will be broken by T-SNE.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:57.310
Now before moving on, let me just quickly describe the similarity function that is most commonly

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.270
used.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:04.910
So the most commonly used similarity function is called cosine similarity.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:09.190
So this is the equation we had from the previous slide.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:16.430
So in cosine similarity, you define the similarity between two vectors u and v as u transpose

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:21.630
v divided by the length, by the Euclidean length.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:25.710
So ignoring the denominator for now, this is basically the inner product between u and

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:26.710
v.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.630
And so if u and v are very similar, their inner product would tend to be large.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:36.910
And this is called cosine similarity because this is actually the cosine of the angle

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:40.110
between the two vectors u and v.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:46.390
So if that's the angle phi, then this formula is actually the cosine of the angle between

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:47.390
them.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:55.230
And so you remember from calculus, I guess that if this is phi, then the cosine of phi

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.310
looks like this.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:02.230
So the angle between them is zero, then the cosine similarity is equal to one, and if

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:08.910
their angle is 90 degrees, the cosine similarity is zero, and then if they're 180 degrees

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:14.870
apart pointing in completely opposite directions, it ends up being negative one.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:17.550
So that's where the term cosine similarity comes from.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:21.350
It works quite well for these analogies and guitars.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:27.950
If you want, you can also use squared distance or Euclidean distance, u minus v squared.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:32.390
Technically this would be a measure of dissimilarity rather than a measure of similarity, so we

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.550
need to take the negative of this.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.510
And this will work okay as well, although I see cosine similarity being used a bit

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.230
more often.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:46.430
And the main difference between these is how it normalizes for the lengths of the vectors

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.430
u and v.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:55.190
So one of the remarkable results about red embeddings is the generality of analogy relationships

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:56.190
they can learn.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:02.350
So for example, it can learn that man is the woman as boy is the girl, because the

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:06.630
vector difference between man and woman, similar to king and queen and boy and girl,

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.350
is primarily just the gender.

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:17.030
It can learn that Ottawa, which is the capital of Canada, is as to Nairobi as the Kenya.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:21.110
So that's the city capital is to the name of the country.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:24.750
It can learn that big is the bigger, it's tall is the taller.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:29.910
And it can learn things like that yen is to Japan, since yen is the currency of Japan,

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.110
as rubo is to Russia.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:38.470
And all of these things can be learned just by running a word embedding learning algorithm

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:43.710
on a large text corpus, since can spot all of these patterns by itself, just by learning

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.790
from very large bodies of text.

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:52.350
So in this video, you saw how red embeddings can be used for analogy reasoning.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:56.390
And while you might not be trying to build an analogy reasoning system yourself as an

00:10:56.390 --> 00:11:01.750
application, this I hope conveys some intuition about the types of featurized representations

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:05.990
or feature like representations that these representations can learn.

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:11.230
And you also saw how cosine similarity can be a way to measure the similarity between

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:16.310
two different words and that things now we've talked a lot about properties of these embeddings

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:17.990
and how you can use them.

